There are several ways to center a `div` horizontally in CSS. Here are the most common and effective methods:

## 1. **Flexbox (Recommended)**
```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}
```
```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="centered-div">This div is centered</div>
</div>
```

## 2. **Text-align (for inline/block elements)**
```css
.container {
  text-align: center;
}

.centered-div {
  display: inline-block;
}
```

## 3. **Margin auto (for block elements with fixed width)**
```css
.centered-div {
  width: 300px; /* Must have a width */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

## 4. **CSS Grid**
```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  justify-items: center;
}
```

## 5. **Absolute positioning with transform**
```css
.centered-div {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
}
```

## Best Practice
**Use Flexbox** (`display: flex; justify-content: center;`) — it's modern, reliable, and widely supported.

### Example:
```html
<div class="wrapper">
  <div class="box">Centered Content</div>
</div>
```

```css
.wrapper {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  height: 100vh; /* For visualization */
}

.box {
  background: #f0f0f0;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  width: 200px;
}
```

💡 **Note**: The `margin: 0 auto` method only works when the element has a defined width. Flexbox and Grid are more versatile and recommended for modern layouts.

Use `margin: 0 auto;` on the div, but only if it has a defined width.  

